  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1106 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSunny and mild daytime weather will continue through Thursday,\nunder a strong upper ridge. 500mb heights will peak around 585dam\nWednesday, prior to the gradual onset of southwest flow aloft on\nFriday. Some medium range guidance, primarily GFS-based, brings\nincreased low/midlevel moisture into the TN Valley on Friday.\nGEFS and ENS mean PWAT values east of the MS River vary\nconsiderably, at 0.8 and 1.3 inches, respectively at 12Z\nSaturday. This available moisture difference is driving a\nrelatively wide spread in the models' prefrontal QPF through\nSaturday night. 01Z NBM deterministic PoPs continue to reflect\nthe drier ENS solution. In any case, expect cloud cover to\nincrease and dewpoints to moderate Friday and Saturday, reducing\nfire weather concerns.\n\nFor Sunday, there is decent consensus regarding the occurrence\nisolated to scattered showers, associated with the passage of a\nprogressive upper level trough and Pacific surface cold front.\nThis event will have little to no impact on the ongoing and\nworsening drought. 12Z LREF quarter inch exceedance probabilities\nthrough Monday morning peak at around 35 percent near the TN\nRiver and near the TN/KY border, and trail off to 20 percent over\neastern AR.  Deterministic QPF is less than a tenth of an inch.\n\nPost-frontal daytime highs will settle down to around 5 degrees\nbelow normal Monday and Tuesday. It appears that the southern\nbranch of the upper jet will become more active next week, though\nmedium range consensus begins to fade with the timing and\namplitude of the approaching waves, decreasing forecast\nconfidence for the travel period prior to Thanksgiving.",
